The [[Leader of the Burawi Republic]] is the directly elected head of state and head of government of Burawa. The office was created by the [[Constitution of Burawa]] in 1932, following the socialist revolution and the replacement of the [[First Republic of Burawa]]. The Leader is elected by popular vote for a five-year term and may be recalled by a seventy-five per cent vote of Parliament under the constitutional recall provisions.

The office was originally conceived as the civilian command post of the socialist state and as the institutional expression of the [[Northern and Central Socialist Party]]'s vanguardist programme. Over time, particularly after the [[1962 Crisis]], its practical powers were narrowed and redistributed among Parliament, the [[Prime Minister of Burawa|Prime Minister]], the [[President of Burawa|President]], and the core ministries. The Leader nevertheless remains the principal executive office of the republic and retains command authority over the armed forces and a veto over acts of Parliament.

== History ==

=== Creation of the office ===

The office was established in 1932, when [[Maana Kadar]] and the NCSP drafted a new constitution and proclaimed the [[Socialist Republic of Burawa]]. Kadar became the first Leader and used the office to direct the early revolutionary state, including the consolidation of the new constitutional order and the mobilisation of the republic during the [[Burawi Domestic War]] and the [[Great Adonian War]].

=== Kadarist era ===

After Kadar's death in 1945, [[Siciid Dhegey]] succeeded him as Leader. Under Dhegey, the NCSP was reconstituted as the [[Kadarist Party]], and the office became associated with an increasingly internationalist style of government. During this period, the Leader's office remained powerful and closely tied to the party-state structure.

=== Post-1962 reforms ===

The [[1962 Crisis]] transformed the constitutional role of the office. The crisis discredited the Kadarist system and prompted the rise of [[Kunciil Hanad]] and the modern [[Socialist Party of Burawa]]. Hanad, then serving as Prime Minister, led the 1962 reforms and brought the Socialist Party to control of the government that year. He later assumed the leadership in 1965, helping stabilise the office while the state moved away from the highly centralised Kadarist model. The reforms that followed reduced the Leader's direct control over domestic administration and established the more limited, tripartite executive structure that remains in place today.

=== Modern era ===

The office remained central to Burawi politics in the twenty-first century. [[Marik Sahamin]] held the office from 2015 until his defeat in the [[2025 Burawi leadership election]]. [[Hancil Thea]] then won the office in the 2025 election, but was recalled from office in March 2026 following the [[2026 Burawi leadership recall vote]]. The subsequent [[2026 Burawi snap election]] was won by [[Muna Xasan]], who became the first woman to hold the office.

== Powers and functions ==

The [[Leader of the Burawi Republic]] is the head of state and head of government of Burawa and serves as the civilian commander of the [[Burawi Armed Forces]]. The office directs the armed forces, supervises [[Public agencies of Burawa|public agencies]] not administered by the core ministries, and may establish new agencies subject to parliamentary confirmation. Within these domains, the Leader may issue executive directives and retains the power of veto over acts of Parliament.

The office's authority was substantially reduced following the [[1962 Crisis]]. The Leader cannot legislate, cannot dissolve Parliament, and cannot call early elections. {{W|Foreign affairs}} are handled through the [[President of Burawa]], while day-to-day legislative management is handled through the [[Prime Minister of Burawa]]. The Leader also nominates the President, who must be confirmed by Parliament, and may be recalled by a seventy-five per cent vote of Parliament. In the event of foreign invasion, the Constitution permits a declaration of {{W|state of emergency|state of emergency}}, which temporarily expands the Leader's authority.

The office was originally conceived as the civilian command post of the socialist state and as the institutional expression of the [[Northern and Central Socialist Party]]'s vanguardist programme. Although that ideological function remains part of the office's constitutional history, its practical role is now centred on military command, the oversight of state agencies, and the exercise of the executive veto.

